{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794554244,"nonce":"mT8MMT+7bk4BK5YsuHEy/+nvPLY=","title":"Passes that still grow with more than what changed: flat-list layout, wide hover rules, a per-call class index","message":"Left over from 2af035d, aaa81e8 and f4a6c9a, which are closed with these in their \"not done\" notes. Measure with crates/blinc_recorder/tests/style_pass_cost.rs (STYLE_NODES, STYLE_TOGGLES, CN_STYLES).\n\n- Layout: taffy's flex pass walks the children of every dirty ancestor. In a flat list of 1,200 children each is a cache hit, and that is about half of a switch toggle at 15,600 nodes (4.5 ms). A virtual list does not pay it. Needs taffy to skip clean children of a dirty parent whose own constraints are unchanged, or lists to be virtual by default (see a31302b).\n- start_all_css_animations builds the class index on every call, about a tenth of a toggle.\n- A rule such as `.list:hover .row` restyles every row it matches on each pass while the list is hovered. An incremental pass would restyle only the nodes reachable from a holder whose state changed, and leave the rest as they were.\n- The first loop of the state pass (elements with an id and state styles) lists every registered id. Fine until rows carry ids.\n- flip.rs and the scrollbar pass loop their rules per element, and the class-change reach loops are O(rules) per change.","files":null}]}